  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The brown-staining cheese polypore is a small, thin woody fungus characterized by its distinctive brown staining on white or cream-colored tissue. Found throughout New England, this species is a wood-decay fungus that plays an important ecological role in breaking down dead hardwood.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Brown staining",
      "description": "Distinctive brown or reddish-brown discoloration on the pore surface and context tissue, especially when bruised or handled",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small, thin fruiting body",
      "description": "Delicate, bracket-like shelf fungi typically less than 5 cm wide with a thin, woody consistency",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Fine pores",
      "description": "Microscopic pores on the underside, appearing as a smooth or finely granular hymenium that stains brown",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This species grows on dead or dying hardwood branches and logs, particularly in moist woodlands and forests throughout New England where decaying wood provides suitable substrate.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The brown staining that gives this fungus its common name intensifies when the fruiting body is damaged or aged, making it an easy species to spot once you know what to look for.",
